# Pepto – Code Workflow (Clone, Push & Pull)

## Overview

This KT explains the end-to-end developer workflow for making code changes in Pepto, starting from cloning repositories to pushing changes, building Docker images, and validating deployments in Dev.

## Repositories Involved

- Application Code Repository (CSCRAWS Pepto / PDS)
- Docker Image Repository (PDS KS / Facade KS)
- Configuration Repository (KS Config)
- Deployment Repository (KS Core Apps – Dev/ITG/Prod)

## Local Setup

- Install Git, Node.js, and VS Code
- Clone required repositories into a structured local folder
- Ensure access to GitHub, Azure DevOps, and Copilot

## Code Change Workflow

1. Clone the repository or open existing local repo.
2. Checkout `k8s-master` branch (source of truth).
3. Run `git fetch` to sync latest changes.
4. Create a feature branch using naming convention: `feature/<name>`.
5. Make required code changes.
6. Validate changes using:
   - `git status`
   - `git diff`
7. Stage changes using `git add`.
8. Commit with meaningful comments.
9. Push feature branch to GitHub.

## Branching Best Practices

- Always branch from `k8s-master`.
- Feature branches do not require approval.
- Same feature branch name must be used across:
  - Code repository
  - Docker repository

## Docker Image Build Flow

- Clone Docker image repository.
- Checkout `master` and pull latest changes.
- Create feature branch with same name as code branch.
- Sync latest code commit into Docker repo.
- Push changes to trigger automated Azure DevOps build.
- Monitor build status and logs in Azure DevOps.

## Deployment to Dev

- Update Helm release files to point to feature branch image.
- Create PR in deployment repo.
- Get Pepto team approval.
- Merge PR to trigger deployment.

## Post-Deployment Validation

- Kubernetes Dashboard: pod status, restarts
- Grafana: CPU and memory usage
- OpenSearch: request and error logs
- S3: data landing verification

## Testing

- Use JMeter to simulate printer traffic.
- Update host/IP for target environment.
- Test core APIs: create project, get project, post message.

## Outcome

This KT enables engineers to confidently perform code changes, push updates, build Docker images, deploy to Dev, and validate functionality end-to-end.
